::GEO_U03_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>An isosceles triangle has congruent sides AB and AC. Which angles are congruent? {
=A. &angle;B and &angle;C#Base angles opposite the congruent sides are congruent.
~B. &angle;A and &angle;B#The vertex angle is not necessarily congruent to a base angle.
~C. &angle;A and &angle;C#The vertex angle is not necessarily congruent to a base angle.
~D. All angles must be 60&deg;#That is true for equilateral triangles, not all isosceles triangles.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>An equilateral triangle has one angle measure of what? {
~A. 45&deg;#45&deg; would not make three equal angles sum to 180&deg;.
=B. 60&deg;#Equilateral triangles are equiangular, and 180&deg; divided by 3 is 60&deg;.
~C. 90&deg;#A triangle cannot have three 90&deg; angles.
~D. 120&deg;#Three 120&deg; angles would exceed 180&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>An isosceles triangle has vertex angle 40&deg;. What is each base angle? {
~A. 40&deg;#The base angles are not necessarily equal to the vertex angle.
~B. 80&deg;#Two 80&deg; base angles plus 40&deg; would total 200&deg;.
=C. 70&deg;#The base angles are equal and sum to 140&deg;, so each is 70&deg;.
~D. 140&deg;#140&deg; is the combined base-angle total.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>An isosceles triangle has base angles of 55&deg;. What is the vertex angle? {
~A. 55&deg;#The vertex angle is not necessarily equal to each base angle.
~B. 110&deg;#110&deg; is the sum of the base angles.
~C. 125&deg;#This does not make the total 180&deg;.
=D. 70&deg;#55 + 55 = 110, and 180 - 110 = 70.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement is true? {
=A. Every equilateral triangle is isosceles.#Equilateral triangles have at least two congruent sides, so they are isosceles.
~B. Every isosceles triangle is equilateral.#Some isosceles triangles have only two congruent sides.
~C. No equilateral triangle is acute.#Equilateral triangles have three 60&deg; acute angles.
~D. Every obtuse triangle is equilateral.#Obtuse triangles cannot be equilateral.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If an equilateral triangle has side length 9, what are the other side lengths? {
~A. 6 and 12#Those side lengths are not all equal.
=B. 9 and 9#All sides of an equilateral triangle are congruent.
~C. 9 and 18#Those side lengths are not all equal.
~D. Cannot be known#The equilateral classification gives enough side information.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>An isosceles triangle has congruent base angles. What can be concluded about opposite sides? {
~A. All sides must be different.#Different sides contradict the isosceles relationship.
~B. The vertex angle is 90&deg;.#No right angle is implied.
=C. The opposite sides are congruent.#Congruent base angles imply the opposite sides are congruent.
~D. The triangle must be scalene.#Scalene means no congruent sides.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which relationship supports solving an isosceles triangle angle problem? {
~A. All triangles have four angles.#Triangles have three angles.
~B. Exterior angles are always 90&deg;.#Exterior angle measures vary by diagram.
~C. All sides are different.#Isosceles triangles have at least two congruent sides.
=D. Base angles are congruent.#Isosceles triangles have congruent base angles.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>An equiangular triangle must also be what? {
=A. Equilateral#A triangle with three congruent angles has three 60&deg; angles and congruent sides.
~B. Scalene#Scalene has no congruent sides.
~C. Right#A right triangle has one 90&deg; angle.
~D. Obtuse#An obtuse triangle has one angle greater than 90&deg;.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>An isosceles triangle has base angle x&deg; and vertex angle 80&deg;. Which equation is correct? {
~A. x + 80 = 180#This omits one base angle.
=B. x + x + 80 = 180#The two congruent base angles plus the vertex angle total 180&deg;.
~C. 2x = 80#This incorrectly sets the base-angle sum equal to the vertex angle.
~D. x + x = 80#This ignores the full triangle angle sum.
}

::GEO_U03_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U03_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>An isosceles triangle has congruent sides AB and AC. Which angles are congruent? {
~A. &angle;A and &angle;B#The vertex angle is not necessarily congruent to a base angle.
~B. &angle;A and &angle;C#The vertex angle is not necessarily congruent to a base angle.
=C. &angle;B and &angle;C#Base angles opposite the congruent sides are congruent.
~D. All angles must be 60&deg;#That is true for equilateral triangles, not all isosceles triangles.
}

